1
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:47,140
CONVERSATION 1
How are you enjoying your new job?

2
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,660
So far, so good.

3
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:54,960
I don't miss having managers who deliver blunt, harsh feedback in the name of efficiency.

4
00:00:54,960 --> 00:01:02,280
From the way you described your last company, no wonder they had a problem with high staff turnover.

5
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:02,680
Yeah.

6
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:06,540
I couldn't wait to get out of there once my contract expired.

7
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:18,880
The problem with a company culture that prizes directness above all else is that it creates a toxic culture of brilliant jerks that drives people out and erodes itself from within.

8
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:28,400
My company's managers tend to be accommodating and kind, overlooking mistakes or issues so as not to hurt feelings.

9
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:33,000
Issues often get ignored there until they build up and reach a crisis point.

10
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,020
That's not surprising.

11
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:42,060
My new company seems to employ a feedback policy that combines compassion and directness.

12
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:49,220
Employees have the power to speak up, give feedback, disagree and discuss problems in real time.

13
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:58,660
It seems to help us to course correct, improve and meet challenges while also building teams that collaborate and care for one another.

14
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:03,180
But that would be based on an atmosphere of mutual trust, wouldn't it?

15
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:07,760
Otherwise, people might interpret feedback as some kind of personal attack.

16
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:08,600
True.

17
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:13,840
Without an atmosphere of trust, feedback can create stress and self-doubt.

18
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:22,360
But I think when we get feedback from someone we trust, we understand that the feedback isn't some kind of personal attack.

19
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:28,340
It's actually a kind of support because it's offered in the spirit of helping us improve.

20
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:34,240
I think sometimes people need to shift their mindsets around how they receive feedback.

21
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:35,540
Yes.

22
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:38,480
Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and grow.

23
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:46,820
It's the basis for healthy parenting, lasting friendships, career development, and so much more.

24
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:55,100
If we shelter our children, friends and colleagues from information that might enrich and enhance their lives, we are not being caring.

25
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:57,060
We're actually doing harm to them.

26
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:05,340
That's exactly right.

27
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:23,440
Q1. How does the woman describe her previous company's culture?

28
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:41,400
Q2. What does the man say about his company's managers?

29
00:03:41,400 --> 00:04:00,000
Q3. What does the woman say the employees in her new company can do?

30
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:17,780
Q4. What does the man say about constructive feedback?

31
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:19,980
CONVERSATION 2
How was your holiday?

32
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:23,600
Not too many other tourists around, were there?

33
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:26,800
No, very few, relatively.

34
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:33,580
But I found myself moving from one accommodation to another, trying to find the perfect place.

35
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:38,760
It made me realize that indecision is a big problem for me.

36
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:43,720
Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot.

37
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:47,340
It seems you suffer from "Fear of Better Options."

38
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:48,700
I've read about it.

39
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:53,020
It describes this loop of indecision as part of our programming.

40
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:58,620
Essentially, we have this tendency to keep stretching out the decision-making process.

41
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:03,140
Because as human beings, we are hard-wired to optimize.

42
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:09,580
We have always looked to get the best things we can as a sort of survival of the fittest.

43
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:14,260
Optimizing isn't the problem, but rather the process that we go through.

44
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:17,020
Well, that makes me feel better.

45
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:24,540
But I think thanks to technology, we can make comparisons more easily and have more access to choice and customization.

46
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:32,860
We can now see what we could have, how we might get it and what others have that we might want.

47
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:38,580
We keep looking over and return to the same options again and again.

48
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:39,600
Yes.

49
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:42,780
Fear of better options offers little benefit.

50
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:44,880
It's an ailment of abundance.

51
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:49,360
You must have choices to have that fear of missing out on better options.

52
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:56,000
Yes, I need to note when I'm worrying about inconsequential things, I guess.

53
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:05,120
If I'm spending too much time worrying over what to have for lunch, I'm robbing myself of the energy to focus on the things that matter.

54
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,360
Exactly.

55
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:11,460
But for more important matters, I think gut instinct might be overrated.

56
00:06:11,460 --> 00:06:16,720
When you have 30 odd options, trusting your gut is not practical.

57
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:25,500
What you need to do is research, have a process, invest time exploring your options, and eliminate as many things as you can.

58
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:39,200
The most toxic part of decision making is going over the same options time and time again.

59
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:57,440
Q5. What does the man say about his holiday?

60
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:16,140
Q6. What does the woman say people tend to do when making decisions?

61
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:35,240
Q7. What has made decision making increasingly difficult?

62
00:07:35,240 --> 00:08:24,480
Q8. According to the woman, what should people do when making important decisions?

63
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:30,580
PASSAGE 1
The role of homework in classrooms is not a new debate.

64
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:35,100
Many parents and teachers are ardent supporters of homework.

65
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:38,500
But do all students benefit from homework?

66
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:52,840
A 2006 research paper suggested some correlation between the amount of homework done by a student and future academic achievement for middle and high school students—but not so much for younger kids.

67
00:08:52,840 --> 00:09:00,640
A Stanford study in 2014 suggested the same was true for students in California's affluent communities.

68
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:06,660
The findings challenged the idea that homework was "inherently good."

69
00:09:06,660 --> 00:09:17,840
The researchers concluded that there was an upper limit to the correlation between homework and achievement, suggesting that high school students shouldn't be doing more than two hours of homework a night.

70
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:25,660
And the most valuable kind of homework for elementary level children was simply assigned free reading.

71
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:33,580
The topic gets more complicated when we talk about the divide between rural and urban communities.

72
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:49,580
Studies found that in remote areas the poor quality or lack of Internet access can put students at a disadvantage, because 70% of teachers in these areas assign homework that requires Internet access.

73
00:09:49,580 --> 00:09:55,940
But one in three households doesn't have Internet.

74
00:09:55,940 --> 00:10:01,520
Experts assert homework requiring the Internet isn't fair.

75
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:11,820
While the debate continues about the effect of homework on academic achievements, there are studies focusing on other benefits of homework.

76
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:32,340
A study in Germany found that homework could have an effect on students' personalities, suggesting that doing homework might help kids to become more conscientious and independent learners.

77
00:10:32,340 --> 00:10:51,000
Q9. What did the 2006 research find about homework?

78
00:10:51,000 --> 00:11:10,180
Q10. What do experts think of homework requiring Internet access?

79
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:28,400
Q11. What conclusion could be drawn from the study in Germany?

80
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:36,920
PASSAGE 2
Robert Goddard, an American born in 1882, is widely regarded as the world's first rocket scientist.

81
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:46,660
At age 27, Goddard published his first book, in which he hypothesized that a rocket launched from Earth could reach the moon.

82
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:52,120
Like many visionaries, the young scientist encountered numerous skeptics.

83
00:11:52,120 --> 00:12:02,300
In January 1920, the New York Times ridiculed Goddard's theory that rockets could be utilized for space exploration.

84
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:08,980
49 years later, Apollo 11 reached the moon, and the famed newspaper published an apology to Goddard.

85
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:17,880
Goddard launched his first rocket from an aunt's farm in his native Massachusetts in March 1926.

86
00:12:17,880 --> 00:12:21,800
His maiden rocket voyage lasted a mere three seconds.

87
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:25,240
It scaled an altitude of only 12 meters.

88
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:29,280
Nonetheless, it was a milestone in rocket science.

89
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:37,620
Goddard later consulted with a weather expert and determined that the climate of New Mexico was ideal for year-round rocket launches.

90
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:47,120
In 1930, Goddard and his family relocated there to a remote valley in the southwest of the country.

91
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:51,020
There he established a laboratory and test range.

92
00:12:51,020 --> 00:12:56,580
However, the ambitious scientist received negligible support from the government.

93
00:12:56,580 --> 00:13:06,320
For four years, wealthy businessman Daniel Guggenheim provided Goddard with an annual $25,000 grant to pursue his dreams.

94
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:10,720
Other rocket enthusiasts also raised funds for him.

95
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:17,780
Over time, Goddard's rockets grew more sophisticated and included the installation of instruments.

96
00:13:17,780 --> 00:13:26,080
In spite of his many successes, Goddard was never able to interest the US military in rocket-propelled weapons.

97
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:41,360
He was granted over 200 patents and continued to pioneer rocket technology until his death in 1945.

98
00:13:41,360 --> 00:14:01,500
Q12. What do we learn about Goddard's idea of using rockets for space exploration?

99
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:20,540
Q13. What does the passage say about Goddard's first rocket launch?

100
00:14:20,540 --> 00:14:37,460
Q14. Why did Goddard move to New Mexico?

101
00:14:37,460 --> 00:15:24,800
Q15. What does the passage say about Goddard's achievements?

102
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:34,960
RECORDING 1
Working for a new venture comes with a lot of risks—that is, instability, unclear responsibilities and the need to be a master of all trades.

103
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:45,220
But the primary benefit is usually the passion and excitement associated with a role in a promising new company.

104
00:15:45,220 --> 00:15:51,240
The person to thank for that passion and excitement is almost always the entrepreneur.

105
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:58,500
There's something about the founder's energy and enthusiasm that infects the rest of the team.

106
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:03,060
The willingness to take risks may inspire others to be more courageous.

107
00:16:03,060 --> 00:16:12,820
The optimism and positivity may motivate people to focus less on trivial and unimportant matters.

108
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:21,100
The celebration of milestones may prompt staff to be more grateful about their own accomplishments and privileges.

109
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:30,880
What becomes set in the firm's culture is a contagious collection of affirmative and positive emotions which are usually shared among the team.

110
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:37,520
Science has already done a good job of proving the results that follow.

111
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:46,080
These include better processes, greater team cohesion, reduced conflict and sharper alertness.

112
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:53,440
But what is yet to be demonstrated is whether the founder's passion leads to increased team performance.

113
00:16:53,440 --> 00:17:07,960
This was recently tested in research, which analyzed the teams of 73 new companies across a range of industries such as IT, medicine and energy.

114
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:21,860
The CEOs were consulted once again years after the initial analysis, and most shared their firm's performance reports, so that their success could be more objectively measured.

115
00:17:21,860 --> 00:17:26,940
Entrepreneurial teams generally progress through three phases.

116
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:40,300
The first is inventing a product or service, the second is founding the venture to sell that product or service, and the third is developing the firm so it continues to grow.

117
00:17:40,300 --> 00:17:48,940
The researchers discovered that when the team is passionate about the third phase—developing a firm—there's a clear link to performance.

118
00:17:48,940 --> 00:18:01,100
But the first phase—a passion for invention—is not a reliable indicator that the firm will still be open for business a few years later.

119
00:18:01,100 --> 00:18:08,840
Likewise, the second—a passion for founding the venture—doesn't necessarily translate into great success.

120
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:22,500
The solution to great team performance stems from a willingness to recruit others who could direct their passion towards the third phase of entrepreneurialism: developing the business.

121
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:30,960
Employing more staff can, in itself, be a risk for an entrepreneur, as is paying them big dollars to attract them.

122
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:49,471
On many occasions, the entrepreneurs reported not paying themselves a wage at all initially in order to cover salaries and expenses.

123
00:18:49,471 --> 00:19:07,100
Q16. What does the speaker say about working for a new venture?

124
00:19:07,100 --> 00:19:26,520
Q17. What has science demonstrated regarding the positive culture of a new venture?

125
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:47,280
Q18. What does the speaker say about entrepreneurs at the initial stage of a new venture?

126
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:57,480
RECORDING 2
Ageing is a curious thing, and people's desire to beat it and death has become an industry worth hundreds of billions of dollars.

127
00:19:57,480 --> 00:20:06,340
Despite the huge investment into research, ageing remains somewhat obscure, although there are certain things researchers do understand.

128
00:20:06,340 --> 00:20:14,460
They know that women tend to have longer life spans, living on average six years longer than men.

129
00:20:14,460 --> 00:20:25,500
No one is really certain of the reason for this, although the speculation centers around the idea that women are more capable of surviving or handling disease than men.

130
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:30,420
For virtually every disease, the effects are greater on men than they are on women.

131
00:20:30,420 --> 00:20:38,520
Some suggest that women's immune systems benefit from their tendency to prioritize and nurture social connections.

132
00:20:38,520 --> 00:20:43,220
But for me, this explanation is hardly convincing.

133
00:20:43,220 --> 00:20:48,060
Researchers also know to an extent what causes ageing.

134
00:20:48,060 --> 00:20:53,340
For 60 years, it was believed that cells would continue to divide forever.

135
00:20:53,340 --> 00:21:02,460
It was only uncovered in relatively recent times that older people's cells divide a smaller number of times than younger people's.

136
00:21:02,460 --> 00:21:07,940
Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever.

137
00:21:07,940 --> 00:21:11,580
Human cells have a limited reproductive ability.

138
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:26,360
To an extent, we can postpone the eventual stop of cells' dividing through nutrition, exercise, good sleep and even relaxation techniques, but we cannot stop the ageing process.

139
00:21:26,360 --> 00:21:34,680
And researchers are yet to answer the ultimate question of ageing: why does the body ultimately fall to pieces?

140
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:46,420
In the opinion of some of the world's best scientific minds on the subject, part of the reason we don't yet have any answer is because many researchers are looking in the wrong direction.

141
00:21:46,420 --> 00:22:01,840
Many public health policymakers believe that the resolution of age-associated disease will tell us something fundamental about the ageing process, but, say some top scientists, "that's completely erroneous."

142
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:09,620
They point to when the diseases of childhood were eliminated, but this did not provide any insight into childhood development.

143
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:22,040
In the same way, the idea that the resolution of age-associated diseases like heart disease and stroke will inform us about ageing is not based on sound science or logic.

144
00:22:22,040 --> 00:22:31,500
At best, if the major causes of death in developed countries were eliminated, this would only add a decade to average life expectancy.

145
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:48,513
But while there is money available to be spent on it, the search to understand the secrets of ageing will be ongoing.

146
00:22:48,513 --> 00:23:06,300
Q19. What do we learn about the possible reason why women tend to live longer?

147
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:24,200
Q20. What is the recent discovery about human cells?

148
00:23:24,200 --> 00:23:38,664
Q21. What do many public health policymakers believe?

149
00:23:38,664 --> 00:23:42,280
RECORDING 3
Good afternoon.

150
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:50,740
In today's talk, we'll discuss how managers can get their staff to do what they are asked.

151
00:23:50,740 --> 00:23:58,540
Much to their frustration, managers often struggle to get their staff to comply with even simple instructions.

152
00:23:58,540 --> 00:24:06,520
Often, they blame their employees: "They don't read emails, they don't listen, they don't care"—that kind of thing.

153
00:24:06,520 --> 00:24:15,480
But according to recent research conducted in Australia, it looks like it's not the employees' fault, but the managers'.

154
00:24:15,480 --> 00:24:21,540
It's easy to understand why people sometimes disobey procedures intentionally.

155
00:24:21,540 --> 00:24:26,280
Occasionally, it's because they're pressured to finish in a short time.

156
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:36,580
At other times, they may disagree with the spirit of the procedure—the effort demanded, the time consumed, the lack of potential effectiveness.

157
00:24:36,580 --> 00:24:44,440
And every now and then, they just don't want to, maybe deliberately or out of stubbornness.

158
00:24:44,440 --> 00:24:50,180
So apart from that, what else gets in the way of procedural compliance?

159
00:24:50,180 --> 00:24:58,020
The research scholars surveyed 152 blue-collar workers from two separate sites in the mining industry.

160
00:24:58,020 --> 00:25:18,540
They asked the workers a range of procedure-related questions, such as whether they found the procedures useful, how confident they felt in their job, how comfortable they were to speak up in the workplace, and how closely they followed any new procedures set by their managers.

161
00:25:18,540 --> 00:25:24,620
They were also asked to rate the extent to which they perceived their supervisors to be helpful.

162
00:25:24,620 --> 00:25:43,360
That last statement was the most instructive because, as the researchers found, there was a remarkably strong correlation between how helpful supervisors were perceived to be and how likely their employees were to follow their directives.

163
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:48,180
Supervisors' helping behavior was found to be motivational in nature.

164
00:25:48,180 --> 00:26:02,760
It increased employees' perception of the likelihood of success in the attainment of job goals, and therefore fostered a willingness to dedicate their effort and ability to their work.

165
00:26:02,760 --> 00:26:08,100
In short, managers should be ongoing role models for the change.

166
00:26:08,100 --> 00:26:12,160
As the saying goes: Do as I do, not as I say.

167
00:26:12,160 --> 00:26:27,200
To affect behavioral change, what's most required is interaction and involvement—the human touch—and, naturally, processes that add value.

168
00:26:27,200 --> 00:26:38,960
Although procedures are designed to guide and support employees' work, employees, it seems, can't always be expected to comply with procedures that are not seen as useful.

169
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:45,320
And of course, managers shouldn't keep resending emails.

170
00:26:45,320 --> 00:27:03,660
They are an effective tool for the sharing of data reports, but they are a hopeless tool if what a manager's desiring is a change in behavior.

171
00:27:03,660 --> 00:27:22,820
Q22. Why are managers often frustrated with their employees?

172
00:27:22,820 --> 00:27:43,060
Q23. Why do employees sometimes disobey procedures intentionally?

173
00:27:43,060 --> 00:28:03,360
Q24. When are employees more likely to follow instructions according to the researchers?

174
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:22,161
Q25. What does the speaker say about emails?
